{"tag":"4Q3T","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15674","version":"1","local":"theorem-1","file":"","label":"thm:wd","number":"1.1","section":"sec-1","order":1,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:wd}\nFor every fixed $0\u003c\\delta\u003c1/4$, as $x\\to\\infty$,\n$\\#\\{p\\text{ prime}:\\ 2x\u003cp\\le5x,\\ P^{+}(p-1)\\le x^\\delta\\}\\ge x^{1-o(1)}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","cites_in_head":["WD|Theorem 1.2"],"labels":["thm:wd"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"erdos824.tex","from":45,"to":48},"hash":"sha256:4cc07a56e1d867d516dd14357a51aaac686d04aaa08b0dae4e19e07cf892eaba","minhash":"","census":"2610.15674v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"WHQQ","kind":"definition","paper":"2610.15674","version":"1","local":"definition-1","file":"","label":"def:aplus","number":"1.2","section":"sec-1","order":2,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{def:aplus}\nHypothesis $\\mathrm{A}^+$: for every $0\u003c\\delta\u003c1/4$ and every $\\eta\u003e0$ there is $y_0\\ge1$ such that for every real $y\\ge y_0$,\n\\[\n \\#\\{p\\text{ prime}:\\ p\\le 5y,\\ P^{+}(p+1)\\le y^\\delta\\}\\ \\ge\\ y^{1-\\eta}.\n\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["def:aplus"],"scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"erdos824.tex","from":52,"to":57},"hash":"sha256:c2965145639b3e198fb00aa8449258d5bbc587816dddbf0f06e48414d712c89b","minhash":"","census":"2610.15674v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"AK2N","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15674","version":"1","local":"theorem-2","file":"","label":"thm:main","number":"1.3","section":"sec-1","order":3,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:main}\nIf $\\mathrm{A}^+$ holds, then for every $\\varepsilon\u003e0$ we have $h(x)\u003ex^{2-\\varepsilon}$ for all sufficiently large $x$. The pairs may moreover be taken with $a,b$ squarefree.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:main"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"erdos824.tex","from":59,"to":61},"hash":"sha256:57b3957c3067a591f5b9c78701b6035c334201989f9b99d3fa6e21bbcfef2a60","minhash":"","census":"2610.15674v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"S8YN","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15674","version":"1","local":"theorem-3","file":"","label":"thm:aplus","number":"1.4","section":"sec-1","order":4,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:aplus}\nAssume that the argument of~\\cite{WD} proving \\Cref{thm:wd} is correct. Then the same argument, modified as described in \\S\\ref{sec:sign}, shows that for every fixed $0\u003c\\delta\u003c1/4$, $\\#\\{p\\text{ prime}:\\ 2x-2\u003cp\\le5x,\\ P^{+}(p+1)\\le x^\\delta\\}\\ge x^{1-o(1)}$; in particular $\\mathrm{A}^+$ holds.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:aplus"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"erdos824.tex","from":65,"to":67},"hash":"sha256:547ea2e4cd093819e35c7c4c765fb987d1fff5b902ea4ed0a44fbff85782fc3c","minhash":"","census":"2610.15674v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"W4YN","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2610.15674","version":"1","local":"corollary-1","file":"","number":"1.5","section":"sec-1","order":5,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"If the argument of~\\cite{WD} is correct, then $h(x)=x^{2-o(1)}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"corollary","item":"statement/corollary","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"erdos824.tex","from":69,"to":71},"hash":"sha256:310df89c8c6881d35374062a392d4a7f990289f1407866ca54c2cc8a210993eb","minhash":"","census":"2610.15674v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"6FUZ","kind":"remark","paper":"2610.15674","version":"1","local":"remark-1","file":"","number":"3.1","section":"sec-7","order":6,"title":"Remark","body_md":"Since $h(x)$ only depends on the integers below $x$, $h(x)=h(\\lceil x\\rceil)$. Hence the theorem for integer $x$, which is the form formalized in Lean, implies it for real $x$: for $0\u003c\\varepsilon\u003c1$, $h(x)=h(\\lceil x\\rceil)\u003e\\lceil x\\rceil^{2-\\varepsilon}\\ge x^{2-\\varepsilon}$. Pollack and Pomerance count pairs with $b\\le x$; the difference is immaterial.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"remark","item":"remark","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"erdos824.tex","from":126,"to":128},"hash":"sha256:271fcdbcf89f39974c2420c370b98411c94e29085873239fb69189a1129c4e99","minhash":"","census":"2610.15674v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
